      "text": "Tesla Delivers 480,126 Cars in Q2—Now Watch Margins & AI Spend\n\nKey takeaways\n- Tesla reported Q2 deliveries of 480,126 vehicles.\n- The real test isn’t volume—it’s whether profitability is recovering.\n- Capital spending on AI and autonomy could weigh on near-term cash generation.\n\nWhat this means for investors\n- Higher deliveries can mask margin pressure if pricing remains aggressive or input costs stay elevated.\n- Look for signs of gross margin improvement and better leverage control as Tesla navigates a tougher competitive landscape.\n- R&D and capex around AI, energy, and autonomy will be a key watch; sustained high spend may keep valuations sensitive to execution signals.\n\nMarket impact\n- Any upside surprise should be weighed against potential headwinds from pricing, tariffs, and slower demand in key regions.\n- Keep an eye on guidance updates for margins, free cash flow, and progress toward autonomy milestones.\n\nStay sharp—share your view: Is Tesla’s margin story turning, or is it still under pressure?",
